CONF6809 -- an OS-9/6809 conformance suite

What this is: a small set of standalone tests, each checking one
documented Microware OS-9 behaviour against what this system actually
does. Every test prints exactly one RESULT line (PASS, FAIL, SKIP or
ERROR); see DOCS/claims for the manual citation behind each one. This
is not a regression suite for any particular emulator -- it exists to
compare real hardware against the documentation.

How to run it, from the OS-9 shell, after mounting this disk as /x0:

    chd /x0
    load procs
    load runb
    chx /x0/CMDS
    runall

The "load" lines matter and are not cosmetic: this account has no PATH
set, so once "chx /x0/CMDS" points command lookup at this disk's
own CMDS/, "procs" (runall's own first real step) and the packed BASIC09
"tally" (which needs RunB to run it) both become unfindable unless they
are already memory-resident first. "load" makes each one resident before
CHX moves away from wherever they normally live, so both keep working
afterward regardless of CHX. Confirmed live: skipping either "load" line
reproduces "Error #216 - Path Name Not Found" partway through the run --
see task-4-report.md.

What it writes: everything lands under /x0 -- RESULTS/report (a RUN
line naming the run, the process list from "procs" so the report
records who ran it, then one RESULT line per test) and whatever a
test's own SCRATCH/ working files were. Nothing outside this disk is
ever touched; no test writes to /dd.

runone reruns the shipped tests one at a time (today: just t01open)
without the "procs" header, appending to the same report -- useful for
rechecking a single test after a partial run. runall and runone always
invoke the same set of tests; the build verifies this.

tally reads RESULTS/report and prints:

    CONF6809 totals: PASS=n FAIL=n SKIP=n ERROR=n

It counts every RESULT line in the file regardless of which run wrote
it, so run it right after the run you care about if you want that
run's own totals.

Redirection note, since the Unix habit is wrong here: on this shell
">>" redirects standard ERROR, not append. ">" fails if the target
already exists. This suite's own procedures use ">+" (append to an
existing file, or create it) for every RESULT-line write, and that
form was confirmed live before it was relied on -- both a two-line
append-in-order check and the negative (">>" puts nothing on the
stdout path) -- see task-4-report.md.

Rebuilding: CMDS/ ships prebuilt so the suite runs with nothing but a
shell. SRC/ carries the exact sources CMDS/ was built from. Running
"rebuild" (after the setup above -- it needs "procs"/RunB resident
same as any other run) reassembles SRC/*.a with this system's own asm
and repacks SRC/*.bas with its own basic09, writing the results into
REBUILT/ -- never into CMDS/ -- then runs the rebuilt suite from
there. CMDS/ is never modified, by any path: a recipient whose asm or
basic09 is present but produces something incompatible -- the case
most worth hearing about -- still ends up with a working prebuilt
CMDS/ *and* whatever REBUILT/ turned out to be, never neither. If this
system has no asm or basic09 at /DD/CMDS, rebuild prints one line
saying so and writes nothing to REBUILT/.

Each run, prebuilt or rebuilt, starts by writing a boundary line --
"RUN prebuilt" or "RUN rebuilt" -- into RESULTS/report before anything
else. The report is append-only, so this is the only thing that lets
a later reader split a report containing both runs back into two.
Please send back the whole report, both runs included (or just the
whole disk image), not a hand-trimmed excerpt.

"rebuild" needs a shell with IF/ELSE/ENDIF (Shell+ or equivalent) to
guard against a missing asm or basic09; runall and runone deliberately
use no conditionals at all, so the core suite runs on the plainest
shell that can read a procedure file. If your shell does not recognise
a keyword, it prints "WHAT?" instead of running that line -- if you
see that from "rebuild", it means your shell lacks IF/ELSE/ENDIF, not
that the disk is broken; runall and runone should never produce it.
